          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My inhaler technique was never checked and I have had two courses of steroids this year. Is that a marker of poor control, and what does guidance describe about stepping up?",
          "answer": "The passages don't say whether two courses of steroids this year means poor control. They do say that exacerbation history is one of the things used to choose a treatment step. The guidance on asthma (*Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy*) says the step is \"chosen from current symptom control and exacerbation history and reviewed rather than fixed.\"\n\nGuidance also says that technique and adherence should be checked before stepping up. The same document says that \"a step-up that compensates for poor technique treats the wrong problem.\" The asthma overview (*Asthma*) adds that poor control is usually a treatment, adherence or inhaler-technique problem rather than evidence of severe disease. The adherence note (*Sticking with asthma treatment*) says technique \"is the first thing usually reviewed when treatment appears to be failing,\" and that a spacer can help with some pressurized inhalers.\n\nThe COPD guidance (*COPD: spirometric diagnosis, group assessment and initial therapy*) also says to review after every exacerbation and to check inhaler technique at each review. The passages don't say which condition applies to you, so the asthma and COPD points may differ for you.\n\nThe passages don't describe the exact steps a clinician would take for your situation. A useful question to take to your clinician is whether your inhaler technique can be checked, and what your recent steroid courses mean for your treatment plan. Do not change or stop any medicine on your own.\n\nSeek urgent care if your breathing becomes much worse, you struggle to speak or walk, or your reliever stops working.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
